 ### [Adjustable Center Point for Voronoi Partitioning](9851-adjustable-center-point-for-voronoi-partitioning.md)
-This method modifies the standard Voronoi partitioning within a barycentric simplex (triangle or tetrahedron) by shifting the central "Y-point" or centroid to arbitrary locations. It solves the problem of inaccurate material interface representation in cases where the uniform distribution assumption fails, such as T-intersections or specific material distributions. Unlike standard Soft Voronoi Decomposition which assumes symmetric sector subdivision, this approach allows the center point $c$ to be adjusted to better weigh materials about the junction, ensuring planar separating surfaces remain consistent.
+This method modifies the standard Voronoi partitioning within a barycentric simplex (triangle or tetrahedron) by shifting the central "Y-point" or centroid to arbitrary locations. It solves the problem of inaccurate material interface representation in cases where the uniform distribution assumption fails, such as T-intersections or specific material distributions. Unlike standard Soft Voronoi Decomposition which assumes symmetric sector subdivision, this approach allows the center point $c$ to be adjusted to better weigh materials about the junction, ensuring planar separating surfaces remain consistent. Choose this variant when material interfaces require asymmetric weighting or when uniform distribution assumptions break down at junctions.
+
+### Tetrahedral Site Insertion
+A strategy to insert new Voronoi sites by forming a regular tetrahedron aligned with the spatial SDF gradient at an existing site, scaling the tetrahedron to avoid crossing the zero-level set [S2]. For each new site $\mathbf{s}'_{i,k}$, compute its SDF value $\phi(\mathbf{s}'_{i,k})$ using a linear approximation based on the original site's SDF value and gradient: $\phi(\mathbf{s}'_{i,k}) \approx \phi(\mathbf{s}_i) + \nabla \phi_i^\top (\mathbf{s}'_{i,k} - \mathbf{s}_i)$ [S2]. The new sites are positioned as $\mathbf{s}'_{i,k} = \mathbf{s}_i + \frac{\rho_i}{4} \mathbf{T}_i \mathbf{d}_k$, where $\mathbf{s}_i$ is the original site, $\rho_i$ is the local spacing, $\mathbf{T}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3\times3}$ is the local frame at $\mathbf{s}_i$, and $\{\mathbf{d}_k\}_{k=1}^4$ are the canonical tetrahedral directions [S2]. The factor $1/4$ scales the tetrahedron to avoid adding new sites that cross the zero-level set [S2].
 
